cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "TCF: Tabular Compact Format"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Leonardo
    family-names: Marques Souza
    email: leonardo.marques.souza@gmail.com
version: "0.8.4"
date-released: "2026-09-01"
repository-code: "https://github.com/LeoPR/TCF"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  TCF (Tabular Compact Format) is a textual compact format for tabular
  string data, with a canonical 2-layer pipeline: OBAT (Online
  Bidirectional Affix Tokenizer) tokenizes via LCP+LCS matching against
  previous strings; HCC (Hierarchical Compositional Coding) provides
  compositional compaction with `~`/`,` operators and auto-naming.
  Pre-1.0 (ADR-0024): format minors are development iterations toward a
  solid 1.0, without rigid cross-version compatibility, since git reproduces
  older versions. The current wire is #TCF.8, with four families selected by
  the shape of the input: single-column, multi-column, records (#TCF.8R, a
  flat rectangular list of dictionaries) and hierarchical. Delta-aware
  pipeline (auto-cadence detection, shape-preserving OBAT hint, HCC seq-RLE
  near-identical detection, auto-detect min_len), a never-worse per-column
  candidate floor (core, raw, dictionary, structural split, domain-bN), and
  optional semantic specs (CPF/CNPJ/IP/ISO date/zero-padded integer). A
  read-only lazy view answers counts, filters and group-by without
  decompressing the whole blob. Validated byte-canonical in 9 synthetic
  datasets (D1-D9) and real-world (Adult Census + TPC-H 9 tables, -33.02%
  weighted; plus UCI wine-quality/Beijing-PM2.5/Online-Retail). Benchmarked
  against csv/jsonl + gzip/brotli/zstd: TCF wins 7/9 datasets.
keywords:
  - tabular-data
  - compression
  - string-encoding
  - affix-tokenizer
  - compositional-coding
  - lcp-lcs
  - delta-aware
